what is a lock-up period entrepreneurs  like the cofounders of Twitter are in  the business of starting companies  growing companies taking those companies  public with an IPO and then becoming  billionaires overnight right well not  quite  when a company shares are initially sold  on the open market the founders and  employees cannot sell their personal  shares for a stretch of time typically 3  or 6 months this is known as the lock-up  period and it's put in place to prevent  large shareholders from flooding the  market and thus depressing the price  it's that old supply and demand law but  there's another slightly less obvious  reason for these lock ups  if a company founder were to cash out as  soon as possible  ie the day the IPO that would hardly  fill the share buying public with much  enthusiasm for the company's future  earnings potential and if you've started  grown and taken your company public  you've probably got a little more faith  in whatever it is you're selling and so  selling those shares can wait a little  longer
 

